WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.440
What's up? Welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners Episode 102 for July 14th, 2026.

00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:11.160
Let's break down today's coolest AI news so anyone can understand it.

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:15.860
Some awesome AI developments today and we're going to make all of it make sense.

00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:17.140
Let's get into it.

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:23.140
You know that feeling when an old book suddenly feels like it belongs on your phone instead of a library shelf.

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:32.680
That is exactly what happened when Ethan Mollick asked an AI called Fable to turn the catalog of ships from Homer's Iliad into a working website.

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:40.820
The result is a clean, clickable site that lets anyone browse Greek warriors their fleets and their leaders without flipping through pages.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:48.960
Think of it like asking a super organized friend to take a long, dusty list and turn it into a museum display you can tap through.

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:57.580
The AI did more than copy words. It sorted the information so ships and leaders appear in clear sections with helpful headings.

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:04.540
It also spotted two real mistakes in an older English translation that careful human readers had missed for years.

00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:13.420
This matters because it shows AI moving beyond quick summaries and into building actual tools for school projects or personal curiosity.

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:20.600
A student could ask something similar and end up with a shareable map or timeline instead of another plain document.

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:26.200
You can visit the site yourself and click around to see how the list feels when it is interactive.

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:35.100
Then open any chatbot and ask it to turn a short passage from a book you like into a simple web page layout you can open in your browser right away.

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:40.740
Okay, now for my favorite part of the show, the deep dive where we look under the hood.

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:46.300
The key idea here is how the AI tracks connections across a whole text at once.

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:52.580
You know how when you're reading a long group chat, your brain still knows who said what even when the topic jumps around.

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:56.920
The AI does something similar but with every word at the same time.

00:01:57.260 --> 00:02:02.880
It breaks the text into tiny pieces and checks how each piece links to the ones before and after it.

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:06.100
Almost like drawing invisible lines between ideas.

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:14.020
Those lines help it decide what becomes a heading, what becomes a list, and where a table would make the information easier to explore.

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:20.860
The same linking process led it compare the old translation against patterns it had learned from Greek texts.

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:26.400
Instead of guessing, it noticed where the English did not line up with the expected structure.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:31.860
That is why it could point out actual mistakes rather than just rewriting the story.

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:39.100
The payoff is that these connection skills turn raw words into something you can actually click and explore on your own.

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:45.420
Let's move to cool stuff and try this where we look at two other interesting updates you can play with today.

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:53.260
First, the very first GPT model has had all its internal numbers released as 80 separate files anyone can look at.

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:59.800
All the numbers that made up the very first GPT model are now online as 80 separate files.

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:04.380
Anyone curious can look at the actual building blocks of early AI.

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:08.800
The files contain the complete set of numbers from that original model.

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:14.240
People can examine the building blocks even though the files will not run it like a chatbot.

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:19.520
The release makes the raw components available for direct inspection by anyone.

00:03:20.180 --> 00:03:25.260
Think of those numbers like the raw ingredients of an early recipe that later models built on.

00:03:25.820 --> 00:03:32.860
Second, researchers noticed that the AI called Claude sounds warmer or stricter depending on the language you use.

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:39.640
Claude changes how warm or strict it sounds depending on whether you're chatting in Hindi, Arabic, or Russian.

00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:43.980
The same model produces different tones across those three languages.

00:03:44.740 --> 00:03:47.080
Researchers are studying why the shifts occur.

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:52.300
The goal is to make future versions more consistent no matter which languages used.

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:56.720
The pattern was noted in public updates from the team behind Claude.

00:03:57.260 --> 00:04:03.080
Imagine the same person changing their tone when they switch from chatting with friends to writing a formal email.

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:08.280
Try opening a chat with Claude in English, then switch to another language you know

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:11.020
and notice how the personality feels different.

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:14.620
Time for quick bits, the short and surprising stories.

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:18.720
The first GPT models numbers are now public in 80 files.

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:24.320
Anyone can examine the actual pieces that made up one of the earliest large language models,

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:26.900
though they will not run like a normal chatbot.

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:31.920
Claude changes how friendly or strict it sounds based on the language you choose.

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:37.460
Researchers are studying the pattern so future versions can stay more consistent across languages.

